description = Single-Player Alpha Build (SPAB). A space game across a whole galaxy: four million stars, each one there to visit, with twelve great powers in two clusters, their wars, small factions and currencies. Fly your ship with Newtonian flight and supercruise, jump between stars along routes or the powers' lanes, and dock at their ports to trade, take war and survey contracts, refit in four module categories, and change ships among forty designs. Insure your ship with a power and wake at its nearest port if you lose it. Rack up too big a bounty, or destroy too many police ships, and the Interstellar Marshals come for you: a neutral force that disables shields and tractors ships out of hyperspace. Worlds turn and orbit in real time, at a twelfth of real size with real gravity, and each one's gravity takes hold below its Kármán line. Land on any airless world: drop out of cruise above it, fly down on your vertical thrusters, and set down on the gear among its hills and craters, with spotlights and night vision for the dark side; a ground scan marks deposits nearby, and first landings sell to cartographers. Surface ports stand on worlds too: starports, settlements, outposts, mining camps, heavy landing fields and forward bases with turrets. Ask the traffic controller for a pad (and wait your turn in the queue behind NPC traffic coming and going), then land, or let an Autopilot Computer fly you in; it also flies cruise to your navigation target. Every port keeps its own market, set by its kind, its world and its people, with stock and demand that drift over the calendar; bigger ports sell more ships and modules, and you can store ships and modules at ports and send for them. Take delivery and ground survey contracts, jump by lining your nose up on the target system's star, and reboot a damaged ship on its oxygen reserve. Simple or Full controls, a radial menu on ~, and Combat and Analysis modes. A settings menu covers graphics, sound and the HUD, rebindable keys and gamepad buttons, nine languages (following Rudolf OS's by default) and accessibility options. Fight pirates and navies with lasers, gatlings, charged mass drivers, missiles and torpedoes, on fixed or gimballed mounts; mine asteroid rings and belts; chart wild systems, survey their worlds and find the ruins of older peoples and their blueprints; claim systems and build colonies. At the galaxy's heart the Maw leads to the Rift, the galaxy upside down (two million stars, an endgame with only about 1,600 scattered havens and whatever colonies you build), where the Riftborn hunt and the Precursors' ruins hold the strongest ships and modules there are. Still to come: mining on worlds, the Core's story and multiplayer. Keyboard and mouse, a controller or touch. Made for Rudolf OS. Duels (0.5.0): two pilots, four ships built for it, an asteroid field, best of three rounds. Phones (0.5.3): touch controls for flying and fighting with several fingers at once, a yaw bar, looking round, and their size, opacity, sensitivity and a left-handed layout in Settings.
